<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>heat-and-temperature</title></head><body><h1>heat-and-temperature</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>First Law of Thermodynamics</li><li>Molecular concept of thermal energy, heat and temperature…</li><li>Thermal Energy, Heat, and Temperature: Cause and Direction of Heat Flow</li><li>Thermal Energy, Heat and Temperature</li><li>Thermal Equilibrium and Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics</li><li>Thermal Equilibrium and Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics</li><li>Thermal Equilibrium and Zeroth Law</li><li>Change of Phases and Latent Heat</li><li>Thermal Equilibrium — Mercury Thermometer</li><li>Quantity of Heat</li><li>Heat and Temperature</li><li>Quantity of Heat</li><li>Temperature Scales</li></ul><h2>First Law of Thermodynamics</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The first law is energy conservation for a thermodynamic system. With heat Q positive into the system and work W positive when done by the system, ΔU=Q−W. Internal energy U is a state function; heat and work are energy transfers across the boundary.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>For a gas expanding against constant pressure, W=PΔV. If 500 J of heat enters and the gas does 180 J of work, then ΔU=500−180=320 J.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>At constant volume, boundary work is zero, so Q=ΔU if no other work is present. In an adiabatic process Q=0 and ΔU=−W. Over a full cycle ΔU=0, hence net Q=net W.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>For a monatomic ideal gas ΔU=(3/2)nRΔT. For 2 mol heated by 10 K, ΔU≈(3/2)(2)(8.314)(10)=249 J, independent of the path between the states.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>A p–V diagram makes work visible: work done by a gas is the area under the process curve, W=∫P dV. Expansion gives positive work under this convention; compression gives negative work.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>State the sign convention before substituting values.</td></tr><tr><td>Q and W depend on path; ΔU depends only on endpoints.</td></tr><tr><td>For a cycle, ΔU=0.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Some books define work on the system as positive and write ΔU=Q+Won; do not mix conventions.</td></tr><tr><td>At constant volume, Q=ΔU only if no other work is present.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The first law accounts for heat and work as energy transfers: ΔU=Q−W when work by the system is positive. Apply the convention consistently to expansion, compression, adiabatic and cyclic processes.</p><hr><h2>Molecular concept of thermal energy, heat and temperature…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Molecular concept of thermal energy, heat and temperature… — Heat and Temperature, Physics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: molecular concept of thermal energy, heat, temperature, cause · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** 1 mole = 6.022×10²³ entities (Avogadro constant, Nₐ). Molar mass (M) is g·mol⁻¹; for a compound M = Σ(atomic masses).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** n (mol) = mass/M = volume(ideal gas, STP)/22.4 L = N/Nₐ.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Limiting reactant is the one consumed first; % yield = (experimental/theoretical)×100.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Heat.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Temperature.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Cause.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Direction of heat flow.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Molecular concept of thermal energy</td></tr><tr><td>• Heat</td></tr><tr><td>• Temperature</td></tr><tr><td>• Cause</td></tr><tr><td>• Direction of heat flow</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Molecular concept of thermal energy" as one blob → ✅ split into: molecular concept of thermal energy, heat, temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ 1 mole = 6.022×10²³ entities (avogadro constant, nₐ). molar mass (m) is g·mol⁻¹; for a compound m = σ(atomic masses)..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Molecular concept of thermal energy, heat and temperature… (Heat and Temperature, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Molecular concept of thermal energy, heat, temperature. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Thermal Energy, Heat, and Temperature: Cause and Direction of Heat Flow</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Thermal energy is the total kinetic energy of the molecules in a substance, which depends on the temperature and the number of molecules.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Heat is the energy transferred between two systems due to a temperature difference. It is a form of energy in transit, not a property of a system.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the molecules in a substance. It determines the direction of heat flow.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The direction of heat flow is always from a region of higher temperature to a region of lower temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Heat flow occurs when there is a temperature difference between two systems in thermal contact.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics establishes that if two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Thermal equilibrium is a state where there is no net heat flow between systems, indicating they have the same temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Thermal expansion is the increase in dimensions of a substance when its temperature is increased, which is often accompanied by heat flow.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>The cause of heat flow is the temperature difference between two systems, which creates a driving force for energy transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Heat transfer mechanisms include conduction, convection, and radiation, each with its own characteristics and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Conduction is the transfer of heat through a material without the movement of the material itself.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Convection is the transfer of heat by the movement of the heated fluid itself.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Radiation is the transfer of heat through electromagnetic waves, which does not require a medium.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>The direction of heat flow is determined by the temperature gradient between two systems.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>In a closed system, heat flow continues until thermal equilibrium is reached, at which point the temperatures of all parts of the system are equal.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Understanding the cause and direction of heat flow is essential for designing and analyzing thermal systems and devices.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Heat always flows from a region of higher temperature to a region of lower temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal equilibrium is a state where there is no net heat flow between systems, indicating they have the same temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics establishes temperature as a well-defined property.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal expansion is the increase in dimensions of a substance when its temperature is increased.</td></tr><tr><td>Heat transfer mechanisms include conduction, convection, and radiation.</td></tr><tr><td>Conduction is the transfer of heat through a material without the movement of the material itself.</td></tr><tr><td>Convection is the transfer of heat by the movement of the heated fluid itself.</td></tr><tr><td>Radiation is the transfer of heat through electromagnetic waves, which does not require a medium.</td></tr><tr><td>The direction of heat flow is determined by the temperature gradient between two systems.</td></tr><tr><td>In a closed system, heat flow continues until thermal equilibrium is reached.</td></tr><tr><td>Understanding the cause and direction of heat flow is essential for designing and analyzing thermal systems and devices.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal contact is necessary for heat transfer between systems.</td></tr><tr><td>Diathermic walls allow heat to pass through, while adiabatic walls do not.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between thermal energy and heat. Thermal energy is the total kinetic energy of the molecules in a substance, while heat is the energy transferred between two systems due to a temperature difference.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the difference between temperature and thermal energy. Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the molecules, while thermal energy is the total kinetic energy.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics correctly to determine thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the formulas for thermal expansion to calculate changes in dimensions.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between conduction, convection, and radiation as mechanisms of heat transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the cause and direction of heat flow in different scenarios.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the formulas for heat transfer using conduction, convection, and radiation.</td></tr><tr><td>Convert between different temperature scales correctly.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the significance of thermal equilibrium in the design and analysis of thermal systems.</td></tr></table><h2>Thermal Energy, Heat and Temperature</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Definition:** A temperature scale is created by identifying two easily reproducible fixed points (usually the freezing and boiling points of water at standard atmospheric pressure), then dividing the interval between them into equal divisions.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Celsius scale (°C):** Introduced by Anders Celsius in 1742. Freezing point of water = 0°C, boiling point = 100°C at 1 atm. Has 100 equal divisions between the fixed points. The SI unit for temperature intervals is the degree Celsius.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fahrenheit scale (°F):** Introduced by Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit in 1720. Freezing point = 32°F, boiling point = 212°F at 1 atm. Has 180 equal divisions. Widely used in the United States.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Kelvin scale (K):** The SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature, named after Lord Kelvin. It is an absolute scale with zero at absolute zero (0 K = −273.15°C). Freezing point of water = 273.15 K, boiling point = 373.15 K. The Kelvin has the same magnitude as the Celsius degree, so ΔT(K) = ΔT(°C).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Conversion formulas:** K = °C + 273.15; °F = (9/5)°C + 32; °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9; K = (°F + 459.67) × 5/9.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Absolute zero:** 0 K = −273.15°C = −459.67°F. The theoretical temperature at which all molecular motion ceases (classical picture). It is unreachable in practice due to quantum zero-point energy. Modern definition: 0 K is the temperature at which the triple point of water occurs at exactly 273.16 K.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Triple point of water:** The unique temperature and pressure at which solid, liquid, and vapor phases of water coexist in equilibrium: 273.16 K (0.01°C) at 611.657 Pa. This is the defining fixed point of the Kelvin scale.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Reaumur scale (°R or °Ré):** Freezing point = 0°Ré, boiling point = 80°Ré. Rarely used today, except historically in some European countries.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Rankine scale (°Ra):** The absolute version of Fahrenheit. 0°Ra = 0 K = −459.67°F. Freezing point of water = 491.67°Ra, boiling point = 671.67°Ra.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Why use Kelvin?** In gas laws (PV = nRT), temperature must be in Kelvin because the relationship P ∝ T is linear only with absolute temperature. At 0 K, the extrapolated volume of an ideal gas would be zero, making physical sense. In Celsius, this would be −273.15°C - a non-zero volume at "zero" temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Gas thermometer:** The most accurate temperature measurement uses a constant-volume gas thermometer. It measures pressure of a fixed volume of gas, which is proportional to absolute temperature. The ideal gas law PV = nRT allows extrapolation to zero pressure for perfect accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Platinum resistance thermometer:** Uses the fact that electrical resistance of metals increases nearly linearly with temperature. R = R₀(1 + αT), where α is the temperature coefficient of resistance. Platinum is used due to its stability and near-linear behavior.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Thermal Energy, Heat and Temperature:** This topic covers the fundamental concepts of thermal energy, heat and temperature in the physics curriculum.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Understanding thermal energy, heat and temperature is essential for solving problems and building higher-level mathematical skills.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Key definitions and theorems related to thermal energy, heat and temperature should be memorized and practiced regularly.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Temperature and heat are NOT the same. Temperature is an intensive property (per unit mass); heat is energy transferred (extensive). A bathtub of 30°C water has more heat than a cup of 80°C water, but lower temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>A temperature difference of 1°C is NOT the same as 1°F. ΔT(°C) = (5/9)ΔT(°F). A 10°C change equals an 18°F change, so 5°C ≠ 5°F.</td></tr><tr><td>Kelvin is NOT written as '°K' - it is simply 'K' (e.g., 300 K, not 300°K). There is no 'degree Kelvin.' The word 'kelvin' is not capitalized when spelled out.</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute zero is NOT just 'really cold' - it is the state where classical thermal motion ceases entirely. At 0 K, only quantum zero-point energy remains, and all real substances have condensed to their ground states.</td></tr><tr><td>The triple point of water (273.16 K) and the freezing point of water (273.15 K) differ by exactly 0.01 K. Confusing these is a common source of error in metrology.</td></tr><tr><td>Carefully distinguish between similar-looking concepts in thermal energy, heat and temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay attention to the conditions under which formulas and theorems apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice with multiple examples to solidify your understanding.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Thermal Energy, Heat and Temperature is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Thermal Equilibrium and Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Thermal Contact: Two systems are in thermal contact when heat can flow between them through a diathermal (heat-conducting) wall. An adiabatic wall prevents heat transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Thermal Equilibrium Definition: Two systems in thermal contact are in thermal equilibrium when there is no net flow of heat between them. Their macroscopic properties (pressure, volume, temperature) remain constant over time. This implies they have the same temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Zeroth Law Statement: If system A is in thermal equilibrium with system B, and system B is in thermal equilibrium with system C, then system A is in thermal equilibrium with system C. This is the transitive property of thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Significance of Zeroth Law: It establishes temperature as a fundamental, measurable property of matter. Without this law, we could not define a universal temperature scale or trust thermometer readings across different systems.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Temperature as State Variable: The Zeroth Law allows us to assign a numerical value (temperature) to every system in equilibrium. All systems in mutual equilibrium share the same temperature value.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Thermometer Principle: A thermometer is system B. When placed in contact with system A, it reaches equilibrium and displays a reading. If two systems give the same reading on the same thermometer, they are in equilibrium with each other — guaranteed by the Zeroth Law.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Historical Context: Though logically prior to the First and Second Laws, the Zeroth Law was formulated last (Fowler, 1931). It was named 'Zeroth' because it underpins all thermodynamics but was recognized after the other laws were established.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Equilibrium vs Steady State: Thermal equilibrium requires NO net heat flow AND uniform temperature throughout. A metal rod with ends at different temperatures may have steady-state heat flow but is NOT in thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Practical Applications: Calibration of thermometers, defining fixed points (ice point, steam point), ensuring consistent temperature measurements across laboratories worldwide.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Thermal equilibrium = no net heat flow = same temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>Zeroth Law provides transitivity: A~B and B~C implies A~C.</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature is a measurable state variable defined by Zeroth Law.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermometers work because of the Zeroth Law.</td></tr><tr><td>Equilibrium requires both thermal contact and sufficient time.</td></tr><tr><td>Zeroth Law was named last but is logically first in thermodynamics.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Thinking equal temperature means equal heat content: Temperature measures average kinetic energy per degree of freedom, not total energy.</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing equilibrium with no heat transfer ever: Equilibrium means no NET flow; microscopic exchanges continue constantly.</td></tr><tr><td>Believing Zeroth Law is trivial: Without it, temperature would lack mathematical foundation and thermometers would be unjustified.</td></tr><tr><td>Assuming equilibrium happens instantly: Real systems require finite time depending on conductivity and heat capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>Mixing up Zeroth and First Law: Zeroth defines temperature; First conserves energy. They address different concepts.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Thermal equilibrium occurs when two bodies in contact have no net heat flow — they share the same temperature. The Zeroth Law states that if A is in thermal equilibrium with B, and B with C, then A is in equilibrium with C. This transitive property justifies temperature as a measurable state variable and validates thermometer use.</p><hr><h2>Thermal Equilibrium and Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Thermal equilibrium is a state in which two or more systems have the same temperature and there is no net heat flow between them.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>When two systems are in thermal equilibrium, they are at the same temperature, and no heat is transferred between them.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics states that if two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>This law establishes temperature as a fundamental property that determines thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>The Zeroth Law is essential for defining temperature and is the foundation for the other laws of thermodynamics.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Thermal contact is required for heat transfer between systems. When two systems are in thermal contact, they can exchange heat energy.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Diathermic walls allow heat to pass through, while adiabatic walls do not allow heat to pass through.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>The Zeroth Law allows us to define temperature using thermometers, which measure the temperature of a system by bringing it into thermal equilibrium with the thermometer.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the molecules in a system.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>The Zeroth Law is crucial for understanding the behavior of thermodynamic systems and is the basis for the definition of temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Thermal equilibrium is a necessary condition for the application of the other laws of thermodynamics.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>The Zeroth Law is essential for the design and analysis of thermodynamic systems and devices.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Thermal equilibrium is a dynamic state where the net heat flow between systems is zero, even though individual molecules may still be moving and transferring energy.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>The Zeroth Law is the foundation for the concept of temperature and is essential for understanding the behavior of thermodynamic systems.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Thermal equilibrium is a state in which two or more systems have the same temperature and there is no net heat flow between them.</td></tr><tr><td>The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics states that if two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other.</td></tr><tr><td>The Zeroth Law establishes temperature as a fundamental property that determines thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal contact is required for heat transfer between systems.</td></tr><tr><td>Diathermic walls allow heat to pass through, while adiabatic walls do not.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermometers measure the temperature of a system by bringing it into thermal equilibrium with the system.</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the molecules in a system.</td></tr><tr><td>The Zeroth Law is essential for defining temperature and is the foundation for the other laws of thermodynamics.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal equilibrium is a necessary condition for the application of the other laws of thermodynamics.</td></tr><tr><td>The Zeroth Law is crucial for the design and analysis of thermodynamic systems and devices.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal equilibrium is a dynamic state where the net heat flow between systems is zero.</td></tr><tr><td>The Zeroth Law is the foundation for the concept of temperature and is essential for understanding the behavior of thermodynamic systems.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between thermal equilibrium and the absence of heat flow. Thermal equilibrium implies equal temperatures and no net heat flow, but individual molecules may still be transferring energy.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the significance of the Zeroth Law in defining temperature and establishing thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the Zeroth Law correctly to determine if two systems are in thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between diathermic and adiabatic walls and their roles in heat transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand how thermometers measure temperature using thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the Zeroth Law to real-world scenarios involving thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>Convert between different temperature scales correctly.</td></tr><tr><td>Explain the relationship between thermal equilibrium and the other laws of thermodynamics.</td></tr></table><h2>Thermal Equilibrium and Zeroth Law</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Two systems are in thermal equilibrium when no net heat flows between them after thermal contact. In ordinary conditions they then have equal temperature, but not necessarily equal internal energy: mass and material also matter.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The zeroth law states that if A is in thermal equilibrium with C and B is in equilibrium with C, then A and B are in equilibrium with each other. This transitive property allows temperature to be measured consistently.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>A thermometer is system C. Allow it to reach a stable reading on body A, then place the same calibrated thermometer on B. Equal stable readings imply A and B have equal temperatures.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Example: a thermometer initially at 20°C placed in warm water gains energy until it and the water reach a common temperature. The final temperature need not equal either initial temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Temperature predicts the direction of spontaneous heat transfer: energy flows from higher temperature to lower temperature until equilibrium, unless work or another process intervenes.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Temperature determines the direction of spontaneous heat flow.</td></tr><tr><td>Thermometry depends on the transitivity of thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>Equal temperature does not imply equal heat capacity or energy.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Heat is energy in transfer, not a stored substance in an equilibrium body.</td></tr><tr><td>Equal temperatures do not imply equal masses or internal energies.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The zeroth law makes temperature comparison possible: systems in equilibrium with the same thermometer are in equilibrium with each other.</p><hr><h2>Change of Phases and Latent Heat</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Phase change is the transition of a substance from one state of matter to another (solid, liquid, or gas).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Latent heat is the amount of heat required to change the phase of a substance without changing its temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>During a phase change, the temperature of the substance remains constant even though heat is being added or removed.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The heat required for a phase change is given by Q = mL, where m is the mass of the substance and L is the latent heat.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>There are six common phase changes: melting, freezing, vaporization, condensation, sublimation, and deposition.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Melting: the process of a solid turning into a liquid (e.g., ice melting into water).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Freezing: the process of a liquid turning into a solid (e.g., water freezing into ice).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Vaporization: the process of a liquid turning into a gas (e.g., water evaporating into steam).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Condensation: the process of a gas turning into a liquid (e.g., steam condensing into water).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Sublimation: the process of a solid turning directly into a gas (e.g., dry ice turning into carbon dioxide gas).</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Deposition: the process of a gas turning directly into a solid (e.g., frost forming on a cold surface).</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Latent heat of fusion (L_f): the heat required to change 1 kg of a substance from solid to liquid (or vice versa) at its melting point.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Latent heat of vaporization (L_v): the heat required to change 1 kg of a substance from liquid to gas (or vice versa) at its boiling point.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Latent heat of sublimation (L_s): the heat required to change 1 kg of a substance from solid to gas (or vice versa).</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>The latent heat values depend on the substance and the phase change involved.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>For water at 1 atm: L_f = 334 × 10³ J/kg, L_v = 2260 × 10³ J/kg.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>The latent heat of vaporization is generally much larger than the latent heat of fusion for a given substance.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>The latent heat of sublimation is approximately equal to the sum of the latent heat of fusion and the latent heat of vaporization.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Phase diagrams show the conditions of temperature and pressure under which a substance exists in different phases.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>The triple point is the unique temperature and pressure at which all three phases (solid, liquid, gas) of a substance coexist in equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>The critical point is the temperature and pressure above which a substance cannot exist as a liquid, no matter how much pressure is applied.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Phase changes are important in many natural phenomena and technological applications, such as weather patterns, climate change, and refrigeration.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>The latent heat of vaporization decreases with increasing temperature and becomes zero at the critical temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>The latent heat of fusion is generally less than the latent heat of vaporization.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Phase changes are accompanied by changes in volume and density.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>The energy required for a phase change is used to overcome intermolecular forces and to do work against external pressure.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Phase change is the transition of a substance from one state of matter to another without a change in temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat is the amount of heat required to change the phase of a unit mass of a substance without changing its temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>During a phase change, the temperature of the substance remains constant even though heat is being added or removed.</td></tr><tr><td>There are six common phase changes: melting, freezing, vaporization, condensation, sublimation, and deposition.</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat of fusion (L_f) is the heat required to change a substance from solid to liquid or vice versa.</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat of vaporization (L_v) is the heat required to change a substance from liquid to gas or vice versa.</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat of sublimation (L_s) is the heat required to change a substance from solid to gas or vice versa.</td></tr><tr><td>The latent heat values depend on the substance and the phase change involved.</td></tr><tr><td>Phase diagrams show the conditions of temperature and pressure under which a substance exists in different phases.</td></tr><tr><td>The triple point is the unique temperature and pressure at which all three phases coexist in equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>The critical point is the temperature and pressure above which a substance cannot exist as a liquid.</td></tr><tr><td>Phase changes are important in many natural phenomena and technological applications.</td></tr><tr><td>The latent heat of vaporization is generally much larger than the latent heat of fusion for a given substance.</td></tr><tr><td>Phase changes are accompanied by changes in volume and density.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between phase change and temperature change. During a phase change, the temperature remains constant, while during a temperature change, the phase remains constant.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand that latent heat is the amount of heat required to change the phase of a substance without changing its temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the formula Q = mL correctly to calculate the heat required or released during a phase change.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between the latent heat of fusion, vaporization, and sublimation.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the significance of the triple point and critical point on phase diagrams.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the concept of latent heat to real-world scenarios involving phase changes.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the changes in volume and density that occur during phase changes.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the concept of phase change to problems involving energy transfer and temperature control.</td></tr><tr><td>Use phase diagrams to determine the conditions under which a substance exists in different phases.</td></tr></table><h2>Thermal Equilibrium — Mercury Thermometer</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Thermal Equilibrium: Two bodies in thermal contact are in thermal equilibrium when they are at the same temperature and no net heat flows between them.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics: If bodies A and B are each in thermal equilibrium with body C, then A and B are in thermal equilibrium with each other. This establishes temperature as a measurable property.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Mercury Thermometer Working: The bulb contains mercury. When placed in contact with a body, mercury absorbs heat and expands. The expansion moves the mercury column up the capillary tube. The height is calibrated to read temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Why Mercury: Mercury has uniform thermal expansion, high boiling point (357°C), low freezing point (-39°C), does not wet glass, and is easily visible due to its shiny surface.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Thermometer Calibration: Two fixed points are used — lower fixed point (ice point, 0°C) and upper fixed point (steam point, 100°C). The interval is divided into 100 equal parts (Celsius scale).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Temperature Reading: Temperature T = ((L_t - L_0)/(L_100 - L_0)) × 100°C, where L_t is length at temperature T, L_0 at ice point, L_100 at steam point.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Thermal equilibrium means equal temperature and no net heat flow.</td></tr><tr><td>Zeroth law establishes temperature as a valid measurable quantity.</td></tr><tr><td>Mercury thermometer works by thermal expansion at thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>Calibration uses ice point (0°C) and steam point (100°C) as fixed points.</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature reading is proportional to mercury column length change.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Confusing thermal equilibrium with thermal expansion: Equilibrium = same temperature; Expansion = volume change due to temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking thermometer measures the body's temperature instantly: It takes time to reach thermal equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>Believing all liquids expand uniformly: Mercury's expansion is nearly uniform; water's is not (anomalous expansion near 4°C).</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing the two fixed points: Ice point = 0°C (lower fixed point), Steam point = 100°C (upper fixed point).</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking thermometer stem temperature doesn't matter: Stem conduction can affect accuracy.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A mercury thermometer works on the principle of thermal equilibrium and thermal expansion. When the thermometer bulb contacts a body, heat flows until both reach the same temperature (thermal equilibrium). Mercury expands uniformly and the level in the capillary tube indicates temperature.</p><hr><h2>Quantity of Heat</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Heat: Energy transferred between bodies due to temperature difference. SI unit: joule (J). Heat flows from higher temperature to lower temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Heat Capacity: C = Q/ΔT is the heat required to raise the temperature of a body by 1 K (or 1°C). Unit: J/K. Depends on mass and material of the body.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Specific Heat Capacity: c = C/m = Q/(mΔT) is the heat required to raise unit mass of a substance by 1 K. Unit: J/(kg·K). Characteristic property of the material.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Molar Heat Capacity: Cₘ = C/n = Q/(nΔT) is heat per mole. Unit: J/(mol·K). Used in thermodynamics and kinetic theory.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Calorimetry Principle: In an isolated system, heat lost by hot bodies equals heat gained by cold bodies. Assumes no heat loss to surroundings.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Latent Heat: Heat absorbed or released during phase change at constant temperature. Q = mL. L_f = latent heat of fusion (solid↔liquid), L_v = latent heat of vaporization (liquid↔gas).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Specific Latent Heat: L = Q/m is latent heat per unit mass. Unit: J/kg. Different for each substance and each phase change.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Heat flows from higher temperature to lower temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>Specific heat capacity c = Q/(mΔT) is a characteristic property of material.</td></tr><tr><td>Water has high specific heat (4186 J/kg·K) — moderates climate.</td></tr><tr><td>During phase change, temperature remains constant despite heat absorption.</td></tr><tr><td>Calorimetry: heat lost = heat gained in an isolated system.</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat of fusion L_f for melting; latent heat of vaporization L_v for boiling.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Confusing heat capacity (for whole body) with specific heat capacity (per unit mass).</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking temperature rises during phase change: Temperature stays constant during melting/boiling.</td></tr><tr><td>Believing heat and temperature are the same: Heat is energy; temperature is degree of hotness.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting to convert units: mass to kg, temperature to K or °C (ΔT same in both), energy to J.</td></tr><tr><td>Assuming calorimetry works without insulation: Heat loss to surroundings causes error.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Quantity of heat is the energy transferred due to temperature difference. Specific heat capacity, heat capacity, latent heat, and calorimetry principle govern heat transfer calculations. Q = mcΔT for temperature change and Q = mL for phase change.</p><hr><h2>Heat and Temperature</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Definition:** A temperature scale is created by identifying two easily reproducible fixed points (usually the freezing and boiling points of water at standard atmospheric pressure), then dividing the interval between them into equal divisions.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Celsius scale (°C):** Introduced by Anders Celsius in 1742. Freezing point of water = 0°C, boiling point = 100°C at 1 atm. Has 100 equal divisions between the fixed points. The SI unit for temperature intervals is the degree Celsius.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fahrenheit scale (°F):** Introduced by Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit in 1720. Freezing point = 32°F, boiling point = 212°F at 1 atm. Has 180 equal divisions. Widely used in the United States.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Kelvin scale (K):** The SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature, named after Lord Kelvin. It is an absolute scale with zero at absolute zero (0 K = −273.15°C). Freezing point of water = 273.15 K, boiling point = 373.15 K. The Kelvin has the same magnitude as the Celsius degree, so ΔT(K) = ΔT(°C).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Conversion formulas:** K = °C + 273.15; °F = (9/5)°C + 32; °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9; K = (°F + 459.67) × 5/9.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Absolute zero:** 0 K = −273.15°C = −459.67°F. The theoretical temperature at which all molecular motion ceases (classical picture). It is unreachable in practice due to quantum zero-point energy. Modern definition: 0 K is the temperature at which the triple point of water occurs at exactly 273.16 K.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Triple point of water:** The unique temperature and pressure at which solid, liquid, and vapor phases of water coexist in equilibrium: 273.16 K (0.01°C) at 611.657 Pa. This is the defining fixed point of the Kelvin scale.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Reaumur scale (°R or °Ré):** Freezing point = 0°Ré, boiling point = 80°Ré. Rarely used today, except historically in some European countries.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Rankine scale (°Ra):** The absolute version of Fahrenheit. 0°Ra = 0 K = −459.67°F. Freezing point of water = 491.67°Ra, boiling point = 671.67°Ra.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Why use Kelvin?** In gas laws (PV = nRT), temperature must be in Kelvin because the relationship P ∝ T is linear only with absolute temperature. At 0 K, the extrapolated volume of an ideal gas would be zero, making physical sense. In Celsius, this would be −273.15°C - a non-zero volume at "zero" temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Gas thermometer:** The most accurate temperature measurement uses a constant-volume gas thermometer. It measures pressure of a fixed volume of gas, which is proportional to absolute temperature. The ideal gas law PV = nRT allows extrapolation to zero pressure for perfect accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Platinum resistance thermometer:** Uses the fact that electrical resistance of metals increases nearly linearly with temperature. R = R₀(1 + αT), where α is the temperature coefficient of resistance. Platinum is used due to its stability and near-linear behavior.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Temperature and heat are NOT the same. Temperature is an intensive property (per unit mass); heat is energy transferred (extensive). A bathtub of 30°C water has more heat than a cup of 80°C water, but lower temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>A temperature difference of 1°C is NOT the same as 1°F. ΔT(°C) = (5/9)ΔT(°F). A 10°C change equals an 18°F change, so 5°C ≠ 5°F.</td></tr><tr><td>Kelvin is NOT written as '°K' - it is simply 'K' (e.g., 300 K, not 300°K). There is no 'degree Kelvin.' The word 'kelvin' is not capitalized when spelled out.</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute zero is NOT just 'really cold' - it is the state where classical thermal motion ceases entirely. At 0 K, only quantum zero-point energy remains, and all real substances have condensed to their ground states.</td></tr><tr><td>The triple point of water (273.16 K) and the freezing point of water (273.15 K) differ by exactly 0.01 K. Confusing these is a common source of error in metrology.</td></tr></table><h2>Quantity of Heat</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Heat Q is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. For a temperature change without phase change, Q=mcΔT, where m is mass and c is specific heat capacity. A temperature difference has the same numerical size in kelvins and degrees Celsius.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Heat capacity C=Q/ΔT applies to the whole object; specific heat c=C/m is per unit mass. A 0.50 kg aluminium block with c=900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ heated by 20 K needs Q=0.50×900×20=9000 J.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>During melting or boiling, temperature remains constant while latent heat changes phase: Q=mL. Melting 0.10 kg of ice at 0°C requires 0.10×334000=33400 J before the resulting water is warmed.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>For an insulated calorimeter, energy conservation gives ΣQ=0: heat lost by hot parts equals heat gained by cold parts. Include the calorimeter and any phase changes when specified.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Example: 0.20 kg water heated by 15 K with c=4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ needs Q=0.20×4200×15=12600 J.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Use mcΔT for sensible heating and mL for phase change.</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature differences can use °C or K; absolute temperature must use K.</td></tr><tr><td>Calorimetry assumes negligible exchange with surroundings unless corrected.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Do not use Q=mcΔT across an interval that includes melting or boiling.</td></tr><tr><td>Celsius is not an absolute temperature for gas-law calculations.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Heat calculations separate temperature change from phase change: Q=mcΔT and Q=mL. Insulated calorimetry follows energy conservation.</p><hr><h2>Temperature Scales</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A temperature scale is a system for measuring temperature based on fixed reference points and equal divisions between them.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The three most commonly used temperature scales are Celsius (°C), Fahrenheit (°F), and Kelvin (K).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>The Celsius scale was introduced by Anders Celsius in 1742, with the freezing point of water at 0°C and the boiling point at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The Fahrenheit scale was introduced by Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit in 1720, with the freezing point of water at 32°F and the boiling point at 212°F at standard atmospheric pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>The Kelvin scale is the SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature, named after Lord Kelvin. It is an absolute scale with zero at absolute zero (0 K = -273.15°C).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>The Kelvin scale has the same magnitude as the Celsius degree, meaning a change of 1°C is equivalent to a change of 1 K.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>The triple point of water is the unique temperature and pressure at which solid, liquid, and vapor phases of water coexist in equilibrium: 273.16 K (0.01°C) at 611.657 Pa. This is the defining fixed point of the Kelvin scale.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Absolute zero is the theoretical temperature at which all molecular motion ceases. It is 0 K, -273.15°C, or -459.67°F. At absolute zero, the internal energy of a system is at its minimum.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>The Kelvin scale is essential for scientific calculations, especially in gas laws, because it is an absolute scale.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>The Reaumur scale and Rankine scale are less commonly used today but were historically significant.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Temperature scales are crucial for understanding and measuring thermal properties and for applying the laws of thermodynamics.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Temperature scales are used in various scientific and engineering applications to measure and control temperature accurately.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>The conversion between different temperature scales is essential for international communication and collaboration in scientific research.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>The Kelvin scale is used in scientific contexts because it is an absolute scale, making it suitable for thermodynamic calculations.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>The Celsius scale is widely used in everyday life and scientific contexts due to its simplicity and intuitive reference points.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>The Fahrenheit scale is primarily used in the United States for everyday temperature measurements.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Temperature scales provide a system for measuring temperature based on fixed reference points and equal divisions.</td></tr><tr><td>The Celsius scale has freezing point at 0°C and boiling point at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>The Fahrenheit scale has freezing point at 32°F and boiling point at 212°F at standard atmospheric pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>The Kelvin scale is an absolute scale with zero at absolute zero (0 K = -273.15°C).</td></tr><tr><td>The Kelvin scale has the same magnitude as the Celsius degree, meaning a change of 1°C is equivalent to a change of 1 K.</td></tr><tr><td>The triple point of water is the unique temperature and pressure at which solid, liquid, and vapor phases coexist in equilibrium: 273.16 K (0.01°C) at 611.657 Pa.</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute zero is the theoretical temperature at which all classical molecular motion ceases: 0 K, -273.15°C, or -459.67°F.</td></tr><tr><td>The Kelvin scale is essential for scientific calculations, especially in gas laws, because it is an absolute scale.</td></tr><tr><td>The Reaumur and Rankine scales are less commonly used today but were historically significant.</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature scales are crucial for understanding and measuring thermal properties and for applying the laws of thermodynamics.</td></tr><tr><td>Conversion between different temperature scales is essential for international communication and collaboration in scientific research.</td></tr><tr><td>The Kelvin scale is used in scientific contexts because it is an absolute scale, making it suitable for thermodynamic calculations.</td></tr><tr><td>The Celsius scale is widely used in everyday life and scientific contexts due to its simplicity and intuitive reference points.</td></tr><tr><td>The Fahrenheit scale is primarily used in the United States for everyday temperature measurements.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between temperature and heat. Temperature is an intensive property, while heat is energy transferred due to a temperature difference.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand that a temperature difference of 1°C is not the same as 1°F. ΔT(°C) = (5/9)ΔT(°F).</td></tr><tr><td>Remember that Kelvin is not written with a degree symbol (°K).</td></tr><tr><td>Understand that absolute zero is not just 'really cold' but the state where classical thermal motion ceases entirely.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between the triple point of water (273.16 K) and the freezing point of water (273.15 K).</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the conversion formulas correctly between different temperature scales.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the importance of using the Kelvin scale in scientific calculations, especially in gas laws.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the triple point of water to define and understand the Kelvin scale.</td></tr></table><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: First Law of Thermodynamics</td><td>The first law accounts for heat and work as energy transfers: ΔU=Q−W when work by the system is positive. Apply the convention consistently to expansion, compression, adiabatic and cyclic processes.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Molecular concept of thermal energy, heat and temperature…</td><td>Molecular concept of thermal energy, heat and temperature… (Heat and Temperature, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Molecular concept of thermal energy, heat, temperature. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Thermal Energy, Heat, and Temperature: Cause and Direction of Heat Flow</td><td>Thermal energy is the total kinetic energy of the molecules in a substance, which depends on the temperature and the number of molecules.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Thermal Energy, Heat and Temperature</td><td>Thermal Energy, Heat and Temperature is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Thermal Equilibrium and Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics</td><td>Thermal equilibrium occurs when two bodies in contact have no net heat flow — they share the same temperature. The Zeroth Law states that if A is in thermal equilibrium with B, and B with C, then A is in equilibrium with C. This transitive property justifies temperature as a measurable state variable and validates thermometer use.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Thermal Equilibrium and Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics</td><td>Thermal equilibrium is a state in which two or more systems have the same temperature and there is no net heat flow between them.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Thermal Equilibrium and Zeroth Law</td><td>The zeroth law makes temperature comparison possible: systems in equilibrium with the same thermometer are in equilibrium with each other.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Change of Phases and Latent Heat</td><td>Phase change is the transition of a substance from one state of matter to another (solid, liquid, or gas).</td></tr><tr><td>9: Thermal Equilibrium — Mercury Thermometer</td><td>A mercury thermometer works on the principle of thermal equilibrium and thermal expansion. When the thermometer bulb contacts a body, heat flows until both reach the same temperature (thermal equilibrium). Mercury expands uniformly and the level in the capillary tube indicates temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Quantity of Heat</td><td>Quantity of heat is the energy transferred due to temperature difference. Specific heat capacity, heat capacity, latent heat, and calorimetry principle govern heat transfer calculations. Q = mcΔT for temperature change and Q = mL for phase change.</td></tr><tr><td>11: Heat and Temperature</td><td>**Definition:** A temperature scale is created by identifying two easily reproducible fixed points (usually the freezing and boiling points of water at standard atmospheric pressure), then dividing the interval between them into equal divisions.</td></tr><tr><td>12: Quantity of Heat</td><td>Heat calculations separate temperature change from phase change: Q=mcΔT and Q=mL. Insulated calorimetry follows energy conservation.</td></tr><tr><td>13: Temperature Scales</td><td>A temperature scale is a system for measuring temperature based on fixed reference points and equal divisions between them.</td></tr></table></body></html>